---
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { pageTitle } from "../data";
import * as code from "../data/snippets";

const contextExample = `// Astro: the value of context={...} is the template's \`context\`
<template flowview={{ title: "Hi", items }} is:raw>
  <h1>{{ context.title }}</h1>
</template>

// JavaScript target: the argument of render()
render({ title: "Hi", items });`;
---

<Layout
  title={pageTitle("Template syntax")}
  description="The complete FlowView template syntax: interpolation, @if, @for, @switch, bindings and the context object."
>
  <div class="page-frame grid gap-12 py-12 md:py-14">
    <header class="grid gap-4">
      <p class="eyebrow">Template syntax</p>
      <h1
        class="max-w-3xl text-4xl font-black tracking-normal text-foreground md:text-5xl"
      >
        Control flow for HTML
      </h1>
      <p class="lede">
        HTML plus a handful of control-flow blocks, interpolation and bindings.
        That is the whole language; the compiler adds no components, layouts or
        partials.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section id="context" aria-labelledby="context-heading" class="grid gap-4">
      <h2 id="context-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground">
        The context object
      </h2>
      <p class="prose-width text-muted-foreground">
        Every template reads its input from a single top-level name, <code
          class="inline-code">context</code
        >. There is no implicit alias and no global scope. Whatever your host
        passes in becomes <code class="inline-code">context</code>.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock code={contextExample} lang="astro / ts" />
    </section>

    <section
      id="interpolation"
      aria-labelledby="interpolation-heading"
      class="grid gap-4"
    >
      <h2 id="interpolation-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground">
        Interpolation
      </h2>
      <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="grid content-start gap-2">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            <code class="inline-code">{"{{ value }}"}</code> is escaped, so markup
            in the value renders as text. It is the default.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            <code class="inline-code">{"{{{ value }}}"}</code> inserts a string verbatim.
            It is for HTML you already trust, and FlowView never sanitizes it. Raw
            interpolation works in element content only, not inside attributes or
            tag names.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            Write <code class="inline-code">\@if</code> or <code
              class="inline-code">\{"{{"}</code
            > to output the marker literally.
            <a class="font-bold text-primary" href="/security"
              >Read the security model →</a
            >
          </p>
        </div>
        <CodeBlock code={code.interpolation} lang="flow" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="if" aria-labelledby="if-heading" class="grid gap-4">
      <h2 id="if-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground">
        @if, @else if, @else
      </h2>
      <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <p class="text-muted-foreground">
          Conditions are JavaScript expressions. The first branch that is truthy
          renders. In the native Rust target, the same conditions are limited to
          a deterministic subset (property paths, literals, comparisons, <code
            class="inline-code">!</code
          >, <code class="inline-code">&amp;&amp;</code>,
          <code class="inline-code">||</code>, <code class="inline-code"
            >??</code
          >).
        </p>
        <CodeBlock code={code.controlFlowIf} lang="flow" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="for" aria-labelledby="for-heading" class="grid gap-4">
      <h2 id="for-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground">
        @for, track, @empty
      </h2>
      <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="grid content-start gap-2">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Iterate arrays, and in the JavaScript target any iterable. <code
              class="inline-code">@empty</code
            > renders when there are no items.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            <code class="inline-code">track</code> is accepted syntax. Today it has
            no runtime effect, because FlowView renders strings and does not diff
            DOM nodes, and the compiler reports it as a warning.
          </p>
        </div>
        <CodeBlock code={code.controlFlowFor} lang="flow" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="switch" aria-labelledby="switch-heading" class="grid gap-4">
      <h2 id="switch-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground">
        @switch, @case, @default
      </h2>
      <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <p class="text-muted-foreground">
          Compare one expression against several cases. <code
            class="inline-code">@default</code
          > handles everything else.
          <a class="font-bold text-primary" href="/capabilities#switch"
            >See it rendered →</a
          >
        </p>
        <CodeBlock code={code.controlFlowSwitch} lang="flow" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section
      id="bindings"
      aria-labelledby="bindings-heading"
      class="grid gap-4"
    >
      <h2 id="bindings-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground">
        Bindings
      </h2>
      <p class="prose-width text-muted-foreground">
        Bindings are evaluated at render time. They are not DOM property
        bindings: <code class="inline-code">[disabled]</code> adds or removes the
        attribute in the HTML string. The boolean attribute set is <code
          class="inline-code">disabled</code
        >, <code class="inline-code">hidden</code>, <code class="inline-code"
          >checked</code
        >,
        <code class="inline-code">selected</code>, <code class="inline-code"
          >required</code
        >, <code class="inline-code">readonly</code>, <code class="inline-code"
          >multiple</code
        > and
        <code class="inline-code">open</code>.
      </p>
      <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        <CodeBlock
          code={code.bindingsDisabled}
          lang="flow"
          label="Boolean attribute"
        />
        <CodeBlock code={code.bindingsClass} lang="flow" label="Class toggle" />
        <CodeBlock code={code.bindingsAttr} lang="flow" label="Any attribute" />
        <CodeBlock
          code={code.bindingsInterpolated}
          lang="flow"
          label="Attribute value"
        />
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
        Event syntax such as <code class="inline-code">(click)</code> belongs to the
        separate <a class="font-bold text-primary" href="/events"
          >Events compiler</a
        >.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="output" aria-labelledby="output-heading" class="grid gap-4">
      <h2 id="output-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground">
        What the compiler produces
      </h2>
      <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <p class="text-muted-foreground">
          The JavaScript target emits a module exporting <code
            class="inline-code">render(context)</code
          >, which returns an HTML string and imports a few escaping helpers
          from <code class="inline-code">@flowview/runtime</code>. The native
          Rust target renders the same language to HTML directly. Both are held
          to one specification.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock code={code.compiledOutput} lang="js" />
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
